I’ll inspect the prompt and translator first, then implement within the accepted AST subset, build a small task-specific K semantics, and validate both execution and proofs. I’ll keep `prove.sh` as the exact reproducible command record.
The prompt’s function is the Brazilian factorial. I’m using a recursive implementation because it stays inside the translator’s small subset and gives K’s reachability prover a clean induction shape: one recursive claim for ordinary factorial and one for the requested product.
Concrete execution now reaches `result(288)` for the prompt example. I’m moving to symbolic proof; the main technical checkpoint is whether the recursive reachability claims close by circularity under arbitrary positive `n`, not merely for a finite test set.
The first symbolic attempt exposed a subtle K prover issue: an intermediate integer continuation unified too broadly with the claim’s destination, so the prover stopped before taking an applicable next rule. I’ve isolated it and am tightening the machine state with function-tagged return events; this preserves concrete behavior while giving recursive claims an unambiguous observation point.
The tagged-return change fixed the premature destination match. The factorial proof is now exploring recursively instead of failing immediately; I also replaced the built-in list call stack with an algebraic stack so symbolic frame matching is deterministic. I’m checking whether circularity closes or whether the claim needs an explicit induction dependency.
Both recursive lemmas now close with `#Top`, and the whole translated program closes with `#Top` when those already-proved lemmas are reused modularly. I’m doing the final reproducibility pass now, including a parser-level equality check that the `solutionProgram` term used by the proof is exactly the term in `solution.mpy`.
Implemented all deliverables:\n\n- [solution.py](/work/solution.py) and generated [solution.mpy](/work/solution.mpy)\n- [semantic.k](/work/semantic.k) and [verification.k](/work/verification.k)\n- Three universal positive-input claims in [spec.k](/work/spec.k)\n- Reproducible commands in [prove.sh](/work/prove.sh)\n\n`./prove.sh` exited 0. Concrete execution produced `result(288)`, the proof term exactly matched `solution.mpy`, and all three `kprove` commands printed `#Top`.\n\nRESULT: KPROVE_PASSED — All required positive proof commands printed #Top and exited successfully.

